```c
char *get_greeting() {
    char *buf = malloc(32);
    strcpy(buf, "hello");
    free(buf);
    return buf;         // returning a pointer to freed memory
}

int main() {
    char *msg = get_greeting();
    printf("%s\n", msg);   // undefined behavior: reads freed memory
}
```

This compiles without a single error on most compilers, and often prints "hello" anyway, because `free` doesn't erase the bytes — it just tells the allocator the block is available for reuse. It stays correct-looking right up until some other allocation reuses that exact block, at which point `msg` silently points at someone else's data. The bug is in `get_greeting`; the visible symptom, if there is one, shows up somewhere unrelated, possibly much later.

C++'s core fix is **RAII** (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization): wrap the allocation in an object's constructor and the deallocation in its destructor, then let the language's own scope rules — which are enforced by the compiler, not by discipline — call the destructor automatically.

```cpp
{
    std::vector<int> v(1000);   // constructor allocates
    // ... use v ...
}   // destructor runs here automatically, frees the buffer
```

Nobody wrote a `free`. The compiler guarantees the destructor runs when `v` goes out of scope — on the normal path, on an early `return`, even when an exception is thrown partway through the block. That last case is the real win: manual `free` calls are notoriously easy to miss on exception/error paths; RAII makes it structurally impossible to skip.
